   "text": "A home Wi-Fi network carries a video stream from the internet to a laptop through a series of steps involving the router, access points, radio channels, and packets. When you start streaming a video on your laptop, the request is sent to the internet, and the video data is broken down into small packets. These packets travel through the internet to your home network.\n\nYour home router acts as the central hub, connecting your devices to the internet. It receives the video packets and forwards them to the access point, which is often the same device as the router or a separate Wi-Fi extender. The access point then broadcasts the video packets on a specific radio channel, which is a frequency in the 2.4 GHz or 5 GHz range, depending on your router's configuration.\n\nThe laptop receives these packets through its Wi-Fi adapter, which listens to the airwaves for incoming data. Each packet contains a unique identifier that allows the laptop to reassemble the video stream in the correct order. As the packets arrive, the laptop's software reassembles them into a continuous video stream, which is then displayed on the screen.\n\nWhen two devices transmit at once, a phenomenon known as a collision can occur. This happens because the radio channels used by Wi-Fi networks are shared."
